<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-ws-06-production-coding">
  <title>Worksheet 6: Production Coding</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>Produce auditable coding records within a documented authorization scope.</li>
      <li>Apply frozen analytic materials consistently across an assigned production batch.</li>
      <li>Preserve source provenance, independent decisions, review history, and unresolved cases.</li>
      <li>Detect coder drift, artifact conflicts, and data-quality problems without silently rewriting earlier work.</li>
      <li>Complete batch-level quality checks and route records according to the pre-specified review plan.</li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This worksheet governs routine production coding after the research lead has
      issued a dated authorization under Worksheet 5. Production coding creates
      research records that may later support substantive analysis, codebook
      development, reliability studies, or computational evaluation. The work must
      therefore remain traceable from the original response and question through every
      coding, review, and adjudication decision.
    </p>

    <p>
      Production does not mean that difficult cases should be forced into ordinary
      categories or that established records may be cleaned up silently. A reliable
      production process preserves ambiguity, OOV and no-match cases, low confidence,
      unresolved decisions, and later overrides. It also records the exact handbook,
      protocol, codebook, vocabulary, question-frame, schema, and software conditions
      that governed the batch.
    </p>

    <p>
      This worksheet does not set one universal batch size, review percentage, or
      acceptance threshold because the current project record does not establish those
      values. The production packet must state them before coding begins. A held-out
      validation batch requires the separate freeze and access controls in
      <xref ref="ch-study-design" text="title"/> and is not authorized merely by this
      worksheet.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-ws-06-status">
      <title>Milestone status</title>
      <p>
        Production authorization is limited to the scope recorded by the research lead.
        It may specify question families, artifact versions, review requirements, data
        locations, and decisions that still require secondary review. Authorization may
        be narrowed or suspended when drift, material changes, or stewardship problems
        arise.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-06-overview">
    <title>Milestone at a Glance</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-06-overview">
      <title>Purpose and output of production coding.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="22%"/>
        <col width="78%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Item</cell><cell>Description</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Primary purpose</cell><cell>Create complete, versioned, auditable human coding records for an approved project batch.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Work mode</cell><cell>Authorized independent coding followed by the pre-specified review, sampling, or adjudication process.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Data</cell><cell>Only the production records identified in the approved batch manifest.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Governing materials</cell><cell>Frozen batch-specific question frames, normative references, protocol, codebook, vocabulary, schema, and authorization record.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Primary deliverables</cell><cell>A locked production record, session and issue logs, integrity report, review records, and a batch disposition.</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Not authorized</cell><cell>Unapproved data access, held-out validation reuse, silent recoding, artifact editing, or external transmission.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-06-prerequisites">
    <title>Prerequisites and Assigned Reading</title>

    <p>
      Begin only after receiving a current production-authorization record and a
      complete batch packet. Review:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li><p>The full ten-step procedure in <xref ref="sec-coding-pass" text="title"/> and the universal checklist in <xref ref="sec-coding-checklist" text="title"/>.</p></li>
      <li><p><xref ref="sec-edge-case-playbook" text="title"/>, <xref ref="subsec-disagreement-notes" text="title"/>, and <xref ref="subsec-disagreement-unresolved" text="title"/> in <xref ref="ch-edge-cases-decisions" text="title"/>.</p></li>
      <li><p><xref ref="sec-practice-readiness" text="title"/> and <xref ref="sec-practice-feedback" text="title"/> in <xref ref="ch-coding-methodology" text="title"/>.</p></li>
      <li><p><xref ref="sec-app-coding-sheet-fields" text="title"/> in <xref ref="app-coding-sheet" text="title"/>.</p></li>
      <li><p><xref ref="sec-app-protocol-workflow" text="title"/>, <xref ref="sec-app-protocol-review" text="title"/>, <xref ref="sec-app-protocol-provenance" text="title"/>, and <xref ref="sec-app-protocol-final-check" text="title"/> in <xref ref="app-protocol-summary" text="title"/>.</p></li>
      <li><p><xref ref="sec-data-access" text="title"/>, <xref ref="sec-data-files" text="title"/>, <xref ref="sec-data-reproducibility" text="title"/>, and <xref ref="sec-data-communication" text="title"/> in <xref ref="ch-data-management" text="title"/>.</p></li>
      <li><p>The question-specific working codebook, normative reference, and goals assigned to every question represented in the batch.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-06-authorization">
    <title>Confirm Authorization Boundaries</title>

    <p>
      Production authorization applies only to the work named in the authorization
      record. Record the limits before opening the batch.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-06-authorization">
      <title>Production-authorization boundaries.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="32%"/>
        <col width="68%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Boundary</cell><cell>Required record</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Coder authorization ID</cell><cell>Record the dated decision issued after supervised coding.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Question scope</cell><cell>List the question families, topics, or specific question IDs the coder is authorized to handle.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Decision scope</cell><cell>Record any fields or decision types that still require secondary review.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Artifact scope</cell><cell>Record the authorized handbook, protocol, codebook, vocabulary, question-frame, and schema versions.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Data scope</cell><cell>Record the approved corpus, storage location, access level, and permitted exports or derivatives.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Review scope</cell><cell>Record which records receive full, sampled, triggered, or other pre-specified review.</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Expiration or reconsideration trigger</cell><cell>Record any date, artifact change, drift event, new question family, or incident requiring renewed authorization.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <warning xml:id="warn-ws-06-scope">
      <title>Do not extend authorization by analogy</title>
      <p>
        Familiar mathematics or a similar question does not authorize coding outside
        the recorded scope. Obtain a new or amended authorization before working with a
        new question family, dataset, access level, or materially changed artifact.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-06-access">
    <title>Materials and Access Rules</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-06-access">
      <title>Resources available and prohibited during production coding.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="50%"/>
        <col width="50%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>You may use</cell><cell>You must not use</cell></row>
        <row><cell>The approved project system, assigned production batch, and authorized derivatives</cell><cell>Unassigned records, raw exports outside your access scope, or personal copies</cell></row>
        <row><cell>The exact question context, stable IDs, normative references, and goals supplied in the packet</cell><cell>Privately reconstructed context or materials from a different question version</cell></row>
        <row><cell>The frozen analytic and storage artifacts named in the manifest</cell><cell>Draft, unlabeled, older, newer, or locally modified substitutes</cell></row>
        <row><cell>The approved research-lead channel for governing-rule, access, and incident questions</cell><cell>Another coder's response-specific record before the assigned review stage</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Machine proposals only when the packet explicitly defines a tool-review condition and preservation layer</cell><cell>Pipeline output during a human-only independent condition</cell></row>
        <row><cell>The approved review records released under the batch plan</cell><cell>Adjudicated or validation labels not authorized for the current stage</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Approved institutional services and devices</cell><cell>External AI, public search, messaging, cloud storage, transcription, or translation services without specific approval</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-06-packet">
    <title>Check the Production Batch</title>

    <p>
      The batch manifest is the operational source of truth for this assignment. Verify
      each item before coding. Do not infer a missing value from the previous batch.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-06-packet-manifest">
      <title>Production batch manifest.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="35%"/>
        <col width="65%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Required item</cell><cell>Value or confirmation</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Batch ID and corpus purpose</cell><cell>Record the stable batch identifier and whether the batch supports production analysis, development, or another approved purpose.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Inclusion boundary</cell><cell>Record the source, date range, course or offering scope, question set, and exclusions supplied by the research lead.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Source and derivative provenance</cell><cell>Record the approved source reference, import or derivative identifier, and transformation version.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Question and response IDs</cell><cell>Confirm every assigned record has stable identifiers and no duplicates or missing rows under the supplied manifest.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Question materials</cell><cell>Record each prompt, representation, goals, question-frame version, and normative-reference version.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Analytic materials</cell><cell>Record handbook, protocol, codebook, vocabulary, and schema versions.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Review condition</cell><cell>Record human-only, tool-review, double-coding, or other assigned condition and the information withheld during the independent pass.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Review plan</cell><cell>Record the pre-specified full, sampled, triggered, or other review rule, reviewer assignment, and release timing.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Escalation and disposition values</cell><cell>Record the exact allowed statuses, routes, and contacts for analytic and stewardship problems.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Work-session requirements</cell><cell>Record the required timing, sitting, interruption, and drift fields.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Submission and lock procedure</cell><cell>Record the approved location, naming rule, due date, integrity check, and lock method.</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Held-out status</cell><cell>Confirm that the batch is not held-out validation, or attach the separate frozen validation protocol that authorizes it.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <warning xml:id="warn-ws-06-stop-manifest">
      <title>Do not code from an incomplete or mixed-version batch</title>
      <p>
        Stop when identifiers, context, versions, review rules, provenance, or storage
        instructions are missing or inconsistent. Do not combine files from different
        packets to create a usable-looking batch.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-06-fields">
    <title>Production Records</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-06-fields">
      <title>Records required during production coding.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="30%"/>
        <col width="18%"/>
        <col width="52%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Record group</cell><cell>Action</cell><cell>Production rule</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Response and episode coding fields</cell><cell>Complete</cell><cell>Apply the assigned full workflow and schema to every response, including response-level stubs when no complete episode exists.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Analytic and routing fields</cell><cell>Complete</cell><cell>Preserve primary and alternate matches, OOV, no match, correctness, coverage, confidence, status, and disposition under the exact allowed values.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Provenance fields</cell><cell>Complete</cell><cell>Record coder, time, batch, source, and every governing artifact version needed to interpret the record later.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Work-session log</cell><cell>Complete as assigned</cell><cell>Record the coding conditions required to examine time, fatigue, interruption, and drift.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Issue and incident log</cell><cell>Complete when needed</cell><cell>Separate analytic uncertainty from privacy, access, identifier, file-integrity, version, and provenance problems.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Independent record</cell><cell>Lock</cell><cell>Preserve the completed first-pass layer before review, machine proposals, comparison, or adjudication are released.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Review and override records</cell><cell>Complete during authorized review</cell><cell>Preserve the original proposal and record the reviewer action, rationale, reviewer, timestamp, disagreement type, and resulting status.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Adjudication record</cell><cell>Complete when required</cell><cell>Store the supported result or unresolved status separately from coder and proposal layers.</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Artifact-change proposal</cell><cell>Complete when needed</cell><cell>Record proposed revisions without changing the frozen materials or reinterpreting earlier records in place.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-06-procedure">
    <title>Production Coding Procedure</title>

    <ol>
      <li><p><em>Confirm authorization.</em> Verify that the batch, questions, artifacts, review condition, and data location fall within the current authorization record.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Verify the batch manifest.</em> Confirm source provenance, stable identifiers, question context, versions, review rules, and lock procedure.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Start a work session.</em> Record the required start time and coding conditions before opening the first response.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Code evidence first.</em> Apply the ten workflow steps in order. Do not let a likely code, correct final answer, or machine proposal determine the extraction.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Preserve difficult cases.</em> Record alternatives, confidence, OOV, no match, policy gaps, and unresolved status rather than forcing completion.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Use the assigned routes.</em> Apply the packet's exact status and disposition values. Do not invent a local route or private exception.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Stop or pause the affected work when necessary.</em> Privacy, access, corruption, identifier, provenance, or mixed-version problems require immediate escalation. Analytic ambiguity may be recorded and routed while unaffected coding continues.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Record suspected drift.</em> When your interpretation changes, preserve earlier records and notify the research lead. Do not revise a completed portion of the batch silently.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>End the work session.</em> Record the stop time, interruptions, fatigue, and unresolved work required by the packet.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Perform the batch integrity check.</em> Verify identifiers, required fields, versions, notes, relations, permitted values, storage location, and absence of identifying information in notes.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Lock the independent production record.</em> Submit through the approved project location before review information is released.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Complete the assigned review condition.</em> Apply full, sampled, triggered, double-coded, or tool-review procedures exactly as specified in the manifest.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Preserve every review action.</em> Do not overwrite the independent record or computational proposal. Link corrections and adjudication to the original record.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Close the batch.</em> The research lead records acceptance, required corrections, unresolved records, drift findings, artifact proposals, and any recoding or authorization decision.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-06-assignment">
    <title>Assignment</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-06-manifest">
      <title>Part A: Complete the Authorization and Batch Manifest</title>
      <statement><p>Record the production-authorization boundaries and every manifest item. Stop if the batch lies outside the authorized scope or contains mixed or missing versions.</p></statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-06-code">
      <title>Part B: Complete the Production Coding</title>
      <statement><p>Apply the full workflow to every assigned response. Complete only the fields authorized for the assigned condition, preserve all evidence and uncertainty, and use the exact packet statuses and routes.</p></statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-06-sessions">
      <title>Part C: Maintain Work-Session and Drift Records</title>
      <statement><p>Record required session information. When suspected drift appears, identify the first affected point, preserve prior rows, stop silent revision, and notify the research lead.</p></statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-06-issues">
      <title>Part D: Maintain the Issue and Incident Log</title>
      <statement><p>Classify each issue as analytic, artifact, privacy, access, file integrity, identifier, version, or provenance. Record the immediate action and the records affected.</p></statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-06-integrity">
      <title>Part E: Perform the Batch Integrity Check</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Confirm that:
          <ul>
            <li>every record has the assigned stable identifiers;</li>
            <li>all required fields and governing versions are present;</li>
            <li>episode numbers and relations are internally consistent;</li>
            <li>low-confidence and unresolved records contain specific notes;</li>
            <li>OOV, no-match, alternatives, and missing goals remain visible;</li>
            <li>no prior record or proposal has been overwritten;</li>
            <li>no identifying information appears in notes or file names;</li>
            <li>the batch remains in the approved location; and</li>
            <li>the submitted record matches the manifest response count and inclusion boundary.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-06-lock">
      <title>Part F: Lock the Independent Batch</title>
      <statement><p>Submit the production record, manifest, work-session log, and issue log through the approved location. Record the submission identifier, assigned integrity confirmation, and lock timestamp supplied by the project procedure.</p></statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-06-review">
      <title>Part G: Complete the Assigned Review</title>
      <statement><p>Review only the records released under the manifest. Preserve the independent value or machine proposal, then record the human action, rationale, reviewer, timestamp, disagreement type, and resulting status.</p></statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-06-close">
      <title>Part H: Complete the Batch Closure Record</title>
      <statement><p>Record accepted records, corrections, unresolved cases, excluded or quarantined records, drift findings, artifact proposals, required recoding, and any change to the coder's authorization.</p></statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-06-escalation">
    <title>Production Escalation Guide</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-06-escalation">
      <title>Required action for production problems.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="28%"/>
        <col width="39%"/>
        <col width="33%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Problem</cell><cell>Immediate action</cell><cell>Record to preserve</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Possible privacy or access incident</cell><cell>Stop affected work and contact the research lead immediately.</cell><cell>What occurred, when, which account, system, or file was involved, and actions already taken.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Unapproved storage or external-service request</cell><cell>Do not transfer the data. Request a project-level approval decision.</cell><cell>Proposed service, intended use, records involved, and permission needed.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Missing, corrupt, duplicated, or conflicting identifiers</cell><cell>Quarantine the affected record and stop dependent coding.</cell><cell>Stable IDs, source reference, observed conflict, and affected rows.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Missing or mixed artifact versions</cell><cell>Pause the affected batch or question family until the assigned versions are restored.</cell><cell>Files received, labels present, expected versions, and records already coded.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Analytic ambiguity or no defensible match</cell><cell>Preserve alternatives, confidence, status, and assigned route; continue unaffected records.</cell><cell>Evidence, current rule, competing readings, and decision blocked.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Recurring codebook, vocabulary, or question-frame gap</cell><cell>Collect comparable cases and submit an artifact proposal rather than a one-case exception.</cell><cell>Original cases, similar cases, near miss, and proposed change.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Suspected coder drift</cell><cell>Stop silent revision and notify the research lead for recalibration, review expansion, or planned recoding.</cell><cell>When the shift appeared, affected question families or fields, and completed record IDs.</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Serious validation-set issue</cell><cell>Follow the frozen validation protocol. Do not revise the method from the held-out case without a documented study decision.</cell><cell>The case, frozen condition, flaw identified, and decision about restart, separation, or exploratory reporting.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-06-deliverables">
    <title>Deliverables</title>

    <p>
      Submit:
      <ol>
        <li>the current production-authorization record;</li>
        <li>the completed batch manifest and inclusion-boundary confirmation;</li>
        <li>the locked independent production record;</li>
        <li>the work-session and coder-drift log;</li>
        <li>the issue, escalation, and incident log;</li>
        <li>the batch integrity report;</li>
        <li>all required review, override, comparison, or adjudication records;</li>
        <li>the artifact-change and recoding proposals;</li>
        <li>the batch closure and authorization decision; and</li>
        <li>the version record below.</li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-06-version-record">
      <title>Version information to preserve with the production batch.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="36%"/>
        <col width="64%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Field</cell><cell>Entry</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>worksheet_version</c></cell><cell><c>ws-06-production-coding-v0.1</c></cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>pass_type</c></cell><cell>Record the assigned production or review condition.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>batch_id</c></cell><cell>Record the stable production-batch ID.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>authorization_id</c></cell><cell>Record the current production-authorization decision.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>source_or_import_id</c></cell><cell>Record the approved source or derivative provenance identifier.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>coder_id</c></cell><cell>Use the assigned project identifier.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>reviewer_ids</c></cell><cell>Record the approved project identifiers for reviewers or adjudicators.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>handbook_version</c></cell><cell>Record the frozen batch version.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>protocol_version</c></cell><cell>Record the frozen batch version.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>codebook_version</c></cell><cell>Record every version represented in the batch.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>vocabulary_version</c></cell><cell>Record every version represented in the batch.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>question_frame_version</c></cell><cell>Record every version represented in the batch.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>schema_version</c></cell><cell>Record the coding, review, and adjudication schema versions.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>review_plan_version</c></cell><cell>Record the pre-specified review rule used for the batch.</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell><c>closed_at</c></cell><cell>Record the final batch-closure date.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-06-checklist">
    <title>Batch Completion Checklist</title>

    <p>
      Before closure, confirm that:
      <ul>
        <li>the coder, questions, dataset, artifacts, and review condition fall within the recorded authorization;</li>
        <li>the batch manifest and source provenance are complete;</li>
        <li>every response and episode uses stable project identifiers;</li>
        <li>all governing versions and required work-session records are present;</li>
        <li>evidence was recorded before interpretation and missing reasoning was not repaired;</li>
        <li>ambiguity, alternatives, OOV, no match, low confidence, and unresolved cases remain visible;</li>
        <li>the exact assigned status and disposition values were used;</li>
        <li>independent records and machine proposals were preserved before review;</li>
        <li>review actions, rationales, reviewers, and timestamps are linked rather than overwritten;</li>
        <li>privacy, access, identifier, file-integrity, version, and provenance issues were handled through the approved route;</li>
        <li>no identifying information or unapproved copy remains in notes, file names, or external systems;</li>
        <li>suspected coder drift and required recoding are documented; and</li>
        <li>the batch closure record states what is accepted, pending, unresolved, excluded, or subject to later revision.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-06-review-closure">
    <title>Batch Review and Closure</title>

    <p>
      The research lead or authorized reviewer applies the review rule recorded before
      coding began. The source materials do not specify one universal review rate;
      therefore, the completed batch is evaluated against the manifest rather than an
      unstated expectation added after results are known.
    </p>

    <p>
      Possible batch outcomes are:
      <dl width="narrow">
        <li><title>Accept</title><p>The batch is procedurally complete and the required review supports its use under the recorded versions.</p></li>
        <li><title>Accept with linked corrections</title><p>Specific reviewed values are corrected in a separate layer while the independent record remains preserved.</p></li>
        <li><title>Expand review</title><p>The observed error, uncertainty, or drift pattern requires review of additional records under a documented rule.</p></li>
        <li><title>Recalibrate the coder</title><p>Production authorization is paused or narrowed until targeted calibration demonstrates restored stability.</p></li>
        <li><title>Quarantine affected records</title><p>Privacy, access, corruption, identifier, or provenance problems prevent ordinary use until resolved.</p></li>
        <li><title>Version an artifact change</title><p>A codebook, vocabulary, question-frame, protocol, schema, or review-rule defect requires a new version and an explicit decision about recoding.</p></li>
        <li><title>Preserve unresolved</title><p>The batch closes with specified cases remaining unresolved because the current evidence or rules do not support a unique decision.</p></li>
      </dl>
    </p>

    <p>
      Closure must state the accepted records, remaining review obligations, unresolved
      cases, artifact proposals, recoding decisions, and any change to authorization.
      Earlier records remain tied to the versions under which they were produced.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
